<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rastatt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rastatt is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the Murg near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. From 1700 Margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-william-margrave-of-baden-baden">Louis William of Baden-Baden</a> built a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palace there, and it remained the margraves' residence until 1771. It hosted the peace conferences of 1714 and 1797–1799, became a fortress of the German Confederation in the 1840s, and in 1849 was the last stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> revolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rastatt.jpg" alt="Rastatt" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carsten Steger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_image_of_Schloss_Rastatt_(view_from_the_southwest).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
